WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.256 --> 00:00:14.518
is a country where artists, intellectuals, and philosophers find inspiration.

2
00:00:15.358 --> 00:00:18.799
For romantics, it is a place where hopes and dreams might come true.

3
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.482
Many people fall under France's enchanting spell, and Amy March was one of them.

4
00:00:25.582 --> 00:00:31.148
Amy found Fred to be everything she had ever hoped for in a suitor: he was from a

5
00:00:31.208 --> 00:00:36.410
very wealthy family, and he was handsome, intelligent, charming, and well groomed.

6
00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:42.232
There was no question that he adored Amy, practically worshipping the ground she walked on.

7
00:00:43.132 --> 00:00:48.874
They went to parties together, walked in the gardens, visited museums, and attended the theater several times.

8
00:00:49.694 --> 00:00:55.176
While she was enjoying these activities and Fred's company, Amy tried to convince herself that he was a

9
00:00:55.216 --> 00:01:01.050
perfect match for marriage (that is, if her parents allowed her to marry at 18, which would be

10
00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:01.671
in one year).

11
00:01:02.631 --> 00:01:04.793
But something kept tugging at Amy's heart.

12
00:01:04.813 --> 00:01:10.356
"Fred is perfect," Amy said to her reflection in the mirror as she got ready for a party

13
00:01:10.396 --> 00:01:10.856
that evening.

14
00:01:10.876 --> 00:01:13.398
"So why do I feel this way?"

15
00:01:14.439 --> 00:01:19.342
Until then, Amy had regarded marriage as a way to secure her position in society.

16
00:01:20.242 --> 00:01:22.804
She did not really believe that love had to be involved.

17
00:01:24.136 --> 00:01:28.497
It was more important to her that she marry the right person rather than a poor artist.

18
00:01:29.558 --> 00:01:34.499
"Love will come later as I grow fond of my husband and respect him," she told herself over

19
00:01:34.559 --> 00:01:35.220
and over again.

20
00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.902
However, after meeting Fred, Amy could not put into practice what she thought she had strongly believed all

21
00:01:41.942 --> 00:01:42.462
these years.

22
00:01:43.542 --> 00:01:44.562
"Could I have been wrong?"

23
00:01:44.883 --> 00:01:47.223
she asked the confused image facing her in the mirror.

24
00:01:48.524 --> 00:01:53.582
For Jo, love was the last thing on her mind as she busied herself in her duties as

25
00:01:53.622 --> 00:01:55.162
a governess to the Kirke girls.

26
00:01:56.243 --> 00:02:00.445
Jo taught them how to sew, bake bread, whistle, play soccer, and love reading.

27
00:02:01.105 --> 00:02:05.207
Her days were so busy with the girls that she had no time to think about the problems

28
00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:07.408
at home, and she didn't mind that.

29
00:02:08.868 --> 00:02:15.511
Evenings were trickier since Jo's responsibilities ended officially at four o'clock, and she had the rest of the

30
00:02:15.571 --> 00:02:17.492
day and evening to do as she pleased.

31
00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:23.723
In order not to fret about Beth, about Laurie, about Amy off in Europe, and about Meg coping

32
00:02:23.763 --> 00:02:29.547
with married life, Jo would either work on her writing or shut herself in the library downstairs and

33
00:02:29.627 --> 00:02:31.248
escape into a world of fiction.

34
00:02:32.689 --> 00:02:37.693
On one such evening Jo had gotten settled in the library and was about to begin reading chapter

35
00:02:37.793 --> 00:02:43.356
four of Jacob's Mysterious Guest, when she heard a man come through the front entrance.

36
00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:48.480
She buried her face in the book, praying that whoever it was would take no notice of her,

37
00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.462
since she was not particularly in a sociable mood.

38
00:02:52.743 --> 00:02:57.146
As luck would have it, the man passed by the open library door without stopping to see who

39
00:02:57.186 --> 00:02:57.826
was inside.

40
00:02:58.887 --> 00:03:04.411
Jo, pulling the book away from her face let out a sigh of relief, but that quickly changed

41
00:03:04.471 --> 00:03:05.111
into a scream.

42
00:03:07.633 --> 00:03:10.956
For there was the man she thought had already gone, standing in the doorway.

43
00:03:10.976 --> 00:03:15.138
"Forgive me, miss," the man apologized profusely.

44
00:03:15.158 --> 00:03:17.100
"I didn't mean to frighten you."

45
00:03:17.808 --> 00:03:23.452
Still shaking from the shock, Jo was unable to speak, but her eyes fell upon his stocking feet,

46
00:03:24.273 --> 00:03:28.056
more specifically upon his left sock, which had a rather large hole in it.

47
00:03:29.337 --> 00:03:33.960
"You see, I thought you were one of the girls trying to hide so she wouldn't have to

48
00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:34.961
go to bed so early.

49
00:03:36.201 --> 00:03:42.506
That's why I took off my shoes so I could scare you, no, not you, but The man

50
00:03:42.586 --> 00:03:44.988
struggled for words in a very strong foreign accent.

51
00:03:45.804 --> 00:03:47.785
"Anyway, I'm very sorry."

52
00:03:48.746 --> 00:03:52.309
Then he gave Jo the warmest and friendliest smile she had ever seen.

53
00:03:53.449 --> 00:03:57.452
By now Jo had caught her breath and was doing her best not to stare at his bare

54
00:03:57.492 --> 00:03:59.594
toe, which was wiggling as he spoke.

55
00:03:59.614 --> 00:04:03.336
"I'm Friedrich Bauer," the man introduced himself.

56
00:04:04.337 --> 00:04:07.879
"Oh, you're the Professor," said Jo, recognizing the name.

57
00:04:07.899 --> 00:04:10.661
"I'm Jo March, the girls' new governess."

58
00:04:10.681 --> 00:04:15.717
"What a pleasure it is to finally make your acquaintance," he said kindly.

59
00:04:16.818 --> 00:04:18.379
The Professor, according to Mrs.

60
00:04:18.439 --> 00:04:23.303
Kirke, was originally from Germany but had come to America a few years ago along with his two

61
00:04:23.423 --> 00:04:24.203
orphaned nephews.

62
00:04:24.844 --> 00:04:29.087
He used to teach at a famous university in Berlin, but now he made his living as a

63
00:04:29.147 --> 00:04:32.150
tutor while caring for his deceased sister's two boys.

64
00:04:33.471 --> 00:04:38.454
Mrs. Kirke said the Professor came from a wealthy and powerful family in Germany, but in his new

65
00:04:38.515 --> 00:04:40.096
country, he was almost penniless.

66
00:04:41.192 --> 00:04:44.813
"Yes, the girls speak of you all the time," the Professor was saying.

67
00:04:44.833 --> 00:04:47.934
"They even showed me what you have taught them.

68
00:04:48.474 --> 00:04:54.956
For example Then to Jo's horror, the Professor began whistling a merry tune she had taught the girls.

69
00:04:58.977 --> 00:05:03.898
Blushing with shame, Jo, who had warned the girls sternly not ever to whistle in public because it

70
00:05:03.938 --> 00:05:10.420
was not ladylike, stuttered, "Well, I the girls just having fun."

71
00:05:11.476 --> 00:05:16.517
With a twinkle in his eye, he continued, "So I taught them a melody I am particularly fond

72
00:05:16.577 --> 00:05:18.138
of. It goes like this."

73
00:05:19.178 --> 00:05:21.859
The Professor began whistling another cheery tune.

74
00:05:23.359 --> 00:05:27.600
This time, Jo could not help laughing as the Professor did a little dance to go with his

75
00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:32.482
song. When his performance ended with a little bow, Jo clapped politely.

76
00:05:36.563 --> 00:05:37.903
I ask what you are reading?"

77
00:05:38.063 --> 00:05:40.264
he asked, peering at the book on her lap.

78
00:05:41.296 --> 00:05:46.180
Jo held it up and said, "I know I should be reading something more educational, but I just

79
00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:47.942
love mysteries and adventures."

80
00:05:48.762 --> 00:05:52.505
Shrugging, the Professor said, "No, you should read what you enjoy.

81
00:05:53.246 --> 00:05:56.529
You are the director of your life, not someone else.

82
00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:02.614
Live in a way that will bring you happiness, not in the way others expect you to live

83
00:06:02.674 --> 00:06:07.197
it." Appreciating his words, Jo asked the Professor to join her for a cup of tea.

84
00:06:07.217 --> 00:06:12.633
"Thank you very much," he said as he sat down in the chair next to Jo.

85
00:06:13.693 --> 00:06:17.394
When he crossed his left leg and saw the hole in his sock, he quickly switched to the

86
00:06:17.434 --> 00:06:17.874
other leg.

87
00:06:18.534 --> 00:06:21.655
Then he let out a loud, hearty laugh.

88
00:06:23.115 --> 00:06:25.636
"Rest assured, Miss March," he said.

89
00:06:25.656 --> 00:06:29.297
"It is not my habit to purposely show my toes to young ladies."

90
00:06:29.997 --> 00:06:33.118
Laughing along with him, Jo took a moment to study the Professor.

91
00:06:33.838 --> 00:06:39.040
He was older than she expected, perhaps in his mid-thirties, which, in comparison with her twenty years, was

92
00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:39.740
quite elderly.

93
00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:41.761
Yet he had a youthful energy.

94
00:06:42.542 --> 00:06:46.185
He also had an inviting warm smile and soft, expressive eyes.

95
00:06:47.066 --> 00:06:52.691
He was not handsome, like Laurie, but his face was interesting, and Jo found herself staring at it

96
00:06:52.851 --> 00:06:54.392
longer than she should have.

97
00:06:54.412 --> 00:06:58.676
"Well, Miss March the Professor said in his strong German accent.

98
00:06:59.376 --> 00:07:01.378
"Please, call me Jo," she interrupted.

99
00:07:01.398 --> 00:07:05.642
"And by the way, if I may say so, your English is excellent, sir."

100
00:07:05.662 --> 00:07:10.225
"Thank you kindly, Jo," he said, pronouncing her name like "Cho."

101
00:07:10.245 --> 00:07:12.826
"Then you must call me Fritz, agreed?

102
00:07:13.246 --> 00:07:17.708
If you like, tomorrow I will prepare a special German hot chocolate drink.

103
00:07:18.308 --> 00:07:20.449
Shall we meet here in the library at the same time?"

104
00:07:21.249 --> 00:07:26.912
"I would love to, Fritz," Jo replied, not understanding why her heart suddenly began to beat faster.
